{
  "image_name": "0602f2f741c9462d1d6062fbb0e8f430.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three ways copper catalysts behave during CO2 reduction and shows how changing the operating mode greatly improves stability. In the conventional case, copper starts as surface nanoparticles, but under static CO2RR the particles dynamically reconstruct: dissolved Cu species move between the surface and solution, particle sizes change, and some material appears to convert into Cu(OH)2. This route is labeled as having poor stability, on the order of about 10 hours.\n\nBy contrast, recoverable operation uses pulsed CO2RR. In one version, copper cycles between dissolved Cu2+ in solution during the anodic step and redeposited Cu particles during the cathodic step. This makes the catalyst effectively recoverable and extends stability to roughly 100 hours. A further strategy adds pyridine, which visibly binds Cu2+ as a Cu2+-pyridine complex. During pulsed operation, this ligand-controlled copper reservoir seems to guide reversible deposition and dissolution more cleanly, suppressing irreversible reconstruction. The catalyst can then be regenerated repeatedly while maintaining activity far longer, with the figure indicating a dramatic increase to about 100 days.\n\nOverall, the main message is that pulsing, especially with pyridine coordination, turns unstable copper restructuring into a reversible, controllable process.",
